WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.377
Now let's run the first SELECT example.

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:06.302
Before you select Run, read the query from top to bottom.

00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:12.347
Notice which columns are requested, which table supplies the data, and how the output is sorted.

00:00:12.347 --> 00:00:15.917
Run it directly in the browser and inspect the result table.

00:00:15.917 --> 00:00:18.782
Look at the column names and the row order.

00:00:18.782 --> 00:00:24.017
The query is not changing anything in the database.

00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:27.477
It is only creating a result from stored rows.

00:00:27.477 --> 00:00:30.327
Then make a connection between each clause and what you see.

00:00:30.327 --> 00:00:32.662
SELECT controls what appears as columns.

00:00:32.662 --> 00:00:36.102
FROM identifies the source table.

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:38.862
ORDER BY changes the order in which the rows are displayed.

00:00:38.862 --> 00:00:47.657
If you want to practice in pgAdmin, copy the same query into the Query Tool and compare the result.

00:00:47.657 --> 00:00:50.592
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:57.522
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:57.522 --> 00:01:00.002
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:00.002 --> 00:01:04.522
In a real setting such as an analyst exploring operational records before building a report,

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:13.267
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:14.007
before discussing syntax.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:20.653
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
